00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.149 align:start position:0%
 
okay so the mcu is easily the most  successful franchise in hollywood often  imitated but never duplicated it  consistently knocks it out the park and  has provided us with incredible stories  and also amazing characters when we look  at the mcu as a whole it's outstanding  what they managed to achieve and whilst  most studios struggle to make a sequel  that feels like it's part of a unified  franchise marvel have crafted over 20  films that all tie together perfectly  over the last decade we've had several  faces introduced and the franchises  catapulted complete unknowns to become  household names now the one at the  center of this is of course tony stark  who has made an icon due to robert  downey jr's performance casting rdj was  a massive risk and if he grew up in the  90s you'd probably remember that he was  arrested several times and that he  battled with addiction in a very public  space whilst he's often looked at  negatively for this you have to give the  guy his props as he's managed to come  through the other side of it and in many  ways he's a shining example of how one  can overcome their own demons this is  why the role of tony stark seems like it  was made for him in the first iron man  film we watch the self-centered arms  dealer happily selling whatever he can  to the army so that those in the middle  east can carry out warfare however upon  seeing the damage that his weapons do  firsthand he decides instead to use his  talents to make the world better and  because of this he's a fascinating  character to watch tony in many ways  represents us all and because of this  many people find him easy to relate to  now though we're not billionaires that  live lavish lifestyles i'm sure many of  you watching this grew up putting  yourself first even to the detriment of  others however as we tend to get older  and our priorities change we also see  how much of a negative effect that this  can have on the world and those around  us tony realizing his mistakes in  crawling out of a literal and  metaphorical cave turns him into the  hero iron man and this rebirth and  resurrection is just as important as his  death now tony's final farewell in  avengers end game feels absolutely  perfect another character going out this  way wasn't initially part of the plan  it's difficult to think of any way that  he could have had a better send off when  we look at the mcu as a whole it's very  much about tony trying to improve the  world around him sacrificing himself so  that others can have a better life the  first film of course shows us through  the idea of him very much giving up the  wealth and fortune that he would make  from dealing weapons however the playboy  persona still has a foot in the game and  this has brought up in avengers when  captain america says that he wouldn't  ever make a sacrifice play tony  definitely shows cap that he would and  come the end of the film he flies up  into the sky with a nuke potentially to  his death  though tony manages to come back he  shows that he would lay down his life if  it came to it and for the next 10 years  or so he's happy to throw himself in a  battle however come the snap things  change though tony survives the battle  with thanos he ends up adrift in space  for several weeks until he's rescued by  captain marvel believing that he's going  to die out there all that he leaves  behind is a recording for peppa potts to  tell her that he loves her however he's  saved and i think that this journey very  much changes him the snap pretty much  took something away from everyone except  for tony whilst most people lost the  ones that they loved peppa survived and  because of this the pair got to have a  daughter together i think that watching  those around him die and knowing that  she could have gone the same way makes  him realize what he almost lost and thus  he holds on to her even tighter tony  quits being iron man and it's a safe  assumption to make that he steps away  from the persona for five years this is  something that he toyed with in the past  and come the end of iron man 3 tony  threw his ark reactor into the ocean to  show that he was done with the role this  was to be with pepper become civil war  we discovered that he couldn't quit tony  had this compulsion to go out there but  after the snap he realized that she was  more important to him than being a hero  he ended up retreating to a country home  and here he raised a family tony had  once more become the selfish and  self-centered person who even refused to  help stephen co when they said there was  a way to fix everything tony just wanted  to be left alone but he knew that deep  down he wouldn't be able to rest if it  meant others wouldn't get to have the  love that he'd found with his family  with peppa's blessing tony returned to  the avengers compound buried the hatchet  with cap and he helped to launch a time  heist to get the infinity stones  although black widow died and nebula was  captured the mission itself was somewhat  a success the avengers managed to bring  everyone back but through the quantum  tunnel came an even more ruthless  version of thanos with the knowledge of  what happens in the aftermath of the  snap he decided to change the plan and  wipe out all of life instead of just 50  of it the final active avengers endgame  is an incredible one and the two sides  throw themselves at each other until  there's just two people left standing  this is thanos and tony now thanos feels  like he's completely unbeatable and  infinity war set him up as an inevitable  force that would power through  everything thrown at him because of his  will to complete this snap guy just had  no chill and after he beats everyone he  manages to get the stones in gauntlet so  that he can carry it out once more now  this is where tony steps in throughout  both infinity war and end game i very  much feel like he knew he would be the  one to stop thanos and that he'd likely  die doing this in infinity war doctor  strange saw over 14 million timelines  and he stated that there was only one in  which they won the wizard even  sacrificed himself and the time stone to  save tony's life and deep down i think  tony knew he'd only do this if it was  necessary become the final battle and  tony asks strange to tell him if they're  in the timeline where they win but  strange very much withholds this  information in case it doesn't happen  tony is a smart guy and to me he clearly  knows that he's going to be the person  who has to go out and make the sacrifice  play no matter how painful it is as  thanos collects the stones tony looks  over the strange who holds up one finger  to symbolize this being the one timeline  tony has one choice and he throws  himself forward and takes the stones  from thanos at this point thanos boasts  and gets one of the biggest fuse when he  realizes that he doesn't have the stones  it's a moment that still makes me fist  punch the air as watching his hubris  lead to his failure never gets old kurta  tony who now has the stones and in  response to thanos saying i am  inevitable tony says i am iron man now  this is an incredible moment that pays  off all the more because it's been  something that tony has added at several  key points he of course said it at the  end of iron man his first film and at  the end of iron man 3 which concluded  his trilogy tony zom has also taken a  lot of focus throughout the mcu and thus  having him holding it up in frame to  carry out the snap means a lot more now  this is because the first time that we  see tony in the mcu we open on his hand  holding a glass of scotch in the back of  the fun bee this very much represents  the self-centered hedonistic attitude he  has and how he doesn't really take  things seriously juxtaposing this tony  holding up his hand to carry out the  snap shows just how far he's come from  being selfish to selfless he perfectly  capitalizes on his arc throughout the  mcu and it's the perfect way to say  goodbye to the character here he  embodies what it means to be a hero and  how the real ones will sacrifice  themselves in order to make things  better for others he saves not only his  daughter and wife but also those who  lost the ones they loved and though the  character dies it's difficult to think  if there's any other way that he could  have gone out in a time where things  fail to stick to landing more times than  not it's actually nice to see a  character get the send-off that they  deserve tony stark very much saved this  universe and to see him being the one  that saves it shows how important he is  to everything robert downey jr goes out  in a triumphant moment with a character  that gave him a second chance and i  absolutely love this scene for all that  it stands for tony very much represents  that even with our less admirable traits  that we can still do good when it comes  down to it and that there lies within us  the ability to be heroic we just need to  make the choice to put others before  ourselves and tony is very much an  inspiration for the audience to keep  this in mind when making difficult  decisions it's an incredible send-off to  the character i think it's definitely  the perfect way for us to say goodbye to  him it makes avengers end game one of  the best comic book movies of all time  and of course love to hear your thoughts  on the scene in the comments below if  you want something else to watch then  make sure you check out our breakdown of  the perfect scene in spider-man into the  spider-verse which will be linked on  screen right now we went over the big  leap of faith what's up danger one and  it's one of our best videos a thing that  we've ever put together hopefully i see  over there right after this but if not  you take care of yourself and i hope you  enjoy the rest of your day i've been  paul you've been the best i'll see you  on the next one take care peace

